"""Original social-enterprise foundations and model choices."""


def build(c, m):
    return c(78, 'Social Enterprise Models and Social Innovation', 'Social Enterprise Models\nand Social\nInnovation',
        'Explain a fictional enterprise mission, trading arrangement and innovation hypothesis while distinguishing proposed value from demonstrated outcomes.', [
        m('Connect mission trading and public benefit', 'Distinguish a social purpose from a business activity and an evidenced benefit.', [
            'Begin with a concrete social concern and a proposed change for an identified group. A venture might sell repair services while creating supported work opportunities, but the sales and the work outcomes are different parts of its purpose. Entrepreneurship can create social as well as financial value; the foundation stage requires support rather than presumed expert independence. Write what the invented enterprise intends to change, what activity it offers and how it expects that activity to contribute. A socially appealing name does not establish that the proposed connection works. [S62]',
            'Separate the user, the person who pays and the person expected to benefit. They may be the same person, or they may have different needs and influence. In an invented repair venture, a shop might pay for maintenance while trainees seek safe learning and reliable work. Identify which promise is made to each party and what resources are needed to honour it. Do not treat a paying customer as the only stakeholder, or assume that someone receiving support has no relevant preferences. These are design questions for the fictional case, not permission to recruit real participants. [S64]',
            'An income arrangement describes how resources enter the enterprise, while an operating arrangement describes how a useful service is produced. A scoping review of food and nutrition enterprises reports varied arrangements and contexts. That diversity motivates asking whether an invented model fits its purpose; it does not establish a model that guarantees survival in every sector. Consider who can access the offer, how staff are supported and which costs recur. If the service depends on unpaid time, record that dependency rather than describing it as free unlimited capacity. [R72]',
            'A defensible mission note keeps intention, activity and observed change separate. State the intended group, the offer, the payer and one observation that would help examine benefit. Include a potential unintended consequence, such as work targets displacing learning time. A partnership study describes competing institutional goals in a limited qualitative setting; it suggests useful questions about tensions, not proof of a universal remedy. Keep the exercise analytical, and identify the qualified role needed before any real employment, safeguarding or contractual arrangement could proceed. [R74]'],
            'An invented repair venture reports that strong sales prove every trainee has gained secure employment. Its shop customers pay promptly, but learning support and trainee experience have not been examined.',
            'Write four entries for mission, user, payer and intended beneficiary. Separate sales evidence from the outcome claim and identify one possible tension.',
            'Sales show trading activity. Secure employment needs different evidence; customers and trainees may require distinct promises, support and review.', ['S62', 'S64', 'R72', 'R74'], [
                ('Mission and sales describe different things.', 'The invented repair venture records sales. What has not thereby been established?', 'Secure employment outcomes for every trainee.', ['That a transaction occurred.', 'That income may help fund operations.', 'That the venture has an offer to examine.'], 'Trading evidence does not by itself demonstrate the intended social change.'),
                ('User payer and beneficiary can differ.', 'A shop pays for repairs while trainees seek supported work. What should the model distinguish?', 'The different roles and promises made to each party.', ['Only the payer matters.', 'Every stakeholder must have identical needs.', 'Trainees cannot have preferences.'], 'A useful model represents who pays, uses and benefits without collapsing their roles.'),
                ('Foundation learning requires appropriate support.', 'What does the introductory enterprise course confer?', 'Supported analytical learning, subject to separate real-world authority.', ['Expert independent trading competence automatically.', 'Permission to employ people without checks.', 'A registered legal enterprise.'], 'A learning pathway does not establish legal or professional readiness.'),
                ('Resource dependencies must remain visible.', 'An invented plan treats volunteer hours as unlimited free capacity. What correction is needed?', 'Record availability, support and replacement dependencies.', ['Assume volunteers can work indefinitely.', 'Delete labour from the operating model.', 'Count goodwill as guaranteed staffing.'], 'Unpaid time still has limits and operating requirements.'),
                ('A model label is not a survival guarantee.', 'How should a sector scoping review inform the fictional model?', 'Use contextual questions about fit and limitations.', ['Select a universally superior label.', 'Guarantee survival from the review headline.', 'Treat every case as a controlled effectiveness trial.'], 'Heterogeneous case-rich evidence does not establish universal superiority.')]),
        m('Compare trading and ownership arrangements', 'Compare alternative enterprise arrangements against mission, resources and accountability.', [
            'Describe an arrangement in ordinary language before choosing a label. An invented venture may sell directly to users, sell to another organisation that purchases access, or combine paid work with a clearly specified support arrangement. Compare who pays, who receives the service and who carries the main costs. Elementary business planning asks for a coherent account of the offer and resources. A classroom model description is not a legal form, a registration decision or a financial forecast; those questions need separate evidence and appropriate qualified advice. [S61]',
            'Ownership and governance concern who can decide and how those decisions are explained. A cooperative identity involves voluntary association and member control, but an enterprise is not made a functioning cooperative merely by adding the word to its name. In the invented comparison, distinguish membership, voting, management and external financing. Record the information a member would need to understand a decision and a possible conflict between an investor preference and the stated social purpose. Do not invent local registration rules or promise that cooperative status protects every participant from loss. [S63]',
            'Use the same comparison headings for each option. Assess the proposed benefit, accessibility, income source, cost, capacity, decision owner and main uncertainty. A sector review can reveal that different arrangements operate in different settings, while leaving the causal explanation of longevity unresolved. Therefore ask whether the case actually supplies the conditions an option depends on. If a buyer must cross-subsidise access for others, identify the assumed surplus and its timing. A generous intention cannot fund an unspecified gap or substitute for evidence of willingness to pay. [R72]',
            'Finish with a conditional choice rather than a slogan. Explain why an option appears suitable under the supplied assumptions, what could make it unsuitable and what verification must precede action. Qualitative partnership evidence highlights the possibility of competing organisational demands, so include how a disagreement about mission would reach a responsible decision role. Preserve the distinction between a proposed structure and an operating arrangement with demonstrated results. The learner output is a reasoned comparison using invented facts; it does not authorise contracts, investment or the creation of a real cooperative. [R74]'],
            'An invented venture compares direct sales with a contract-funded service. The contract would increase income but remove supported learning time; a cooperative label is proposed without member decision arrangements.',
            'Compare both options under six common headings. Recommend a conditional option and name a governance question that must be resolved.',
            'Income, mission, capacity and control must be considered together. A label does not settle ownership or remove the conflict about supported learning.', ['S61', 'S63', 'R72', 'R74'], [
                ('Descriptions should precede legal labels.', 'What is a useful first description of the invented venture option?', 'Who pays for what service and with which resources.', ['A claim of registration without records.', 'A fashionable label alone.', 'An assumed tax exemption.'], 'A model needs an operating explanation before legal status can be considered.'),
                ('Governance requires actual decision arrangements.', 'What is missing from the fictional cooperative label?', 'Defined membership and accountable decision arrangements.', ['A guaranteed return to all investors.', 'An exemption from all local rules.', 'Proof that no conflict can occur.'], 'A name does not establish member control or a functioning process.'),
                ('Options need common criteria.', 'Which comparison supports a reviewable fictional decision?', 'Mission, access, income, costs, capacity and control for each option.', ['Income for one option and slogans for the other.', 'Only the biggest projected revenue.', 'A ranking with all uncertainties hidden.'], 'Consistent criteria expose trade-offs rather than concealing them.'),
                ('Cross-subsidy depends on an actual surplus.', 'An invented plan promises free access funded by sales. What must be examined?', 'The assumed surplus, timing and reliability of sales.', ['Good intentions guarantee enough cash.', 'All revenue can be spent twice.', 'No service costs need recording.'], 'Revenue after relevant costs and its availability matter for a subsidy promise.'),
                ('Conditional recommendations identify limits.', 'Which fictional recommendation is strongest?', 'Prefer an option under stated conditions with a defined verification step.', ['Sign a real contract through coursework.', 'Guarantee success because an article was cited.', 'Ignore the mission conflict.'], 'A bounded choice explains both its rationale and conditions.')]),
        m('Develop and test an innovation hypothesis', 'Frame a small fictional learning test with a relevant observation and stopping condition.', [
            'An innovation is a proposed change in how value is created, delivered or supported; novelty alone does not make it useful. Start with the obstacle in the invented service and explain how a change might address it. An accessible booking route could reduce one task barrier without fixing insufficient repair capacity. The entrepreneurship progression emphasises opportunity, resources and action together. Keep the learning hypothesis narrow enough to examine, including the condition that must hold between the change and the intended result. A new application or a distinctive brand is not itself evidence of social benefit. [S62]',
            'Design a low-risk classroom test using invented records or role descriptions. State the question, the proposed change, the observation and the decision it could inform. For example, compare two fictional service descriptions for clarity about price, access and response ownership. Do not recruit actual service users or ask learners to expose personal circumstances. A practical assessment tool may help structure reasoning, but design-science usability work does not prove that a proposed venture improves lives. An output proxy should be labelled for what it measures, especially when the venture has not begun operating. [R71]',
            'Include an observation that could challenge the preferred idea. If the invented booking change increases requests but leaves more people without a response, the team must reconsider capacity rather than declare success from traffic alone. Record the denominator, time period and missing information. Proportionate impact guidance encourages learning and stakeholder relevance; it does not require collecting every possible variable. Explain what the fictional observation can support and what remains unknown about longer-term outcomes or people who never reached the service. The quality of the test depends on an honest decision connection. [S64]',
            'Write the next step as continue, modify or pause under stated conditions. Assign a responsible review role and preserve the original hypothesis so changes remain visible. A pilot does not justify expansion before operating requirements are checked, and a classroom exercise does not authorise a real service launch. Consider whether the adjustment protects the mission as well as the trading arrangement. The final learning note should describe an observation and a justified decision, without presenting an untested idea, an attractive prototype or an isolated positive comment as established enterprise effectiveness. [S62]'],
            'An invented booking prototype doubles requests but has no extra processing capacity. The team calls clicks proof of inclusion and plans immediate expansion without a review point.',
            'Write a hypothesis, a defined observation, a possible disconfirming result and a conditional continue/modify/pause decision.',
            'More requests do not establish usable access. Include completed responses and unmet demand, then connect the observation to capacity and a bounded decision.', ['S62', 'R71', 'S64'], [
                ('Novelty is not demonstrated usefulness.', 'What must accompany the invented new booking application?', 'A plausible access mechanism and evidence question.', ['A claim that new technology always creates benefit.', 'A promise that branding eliminates barriers.', 'An assumption that requests are completed services.'], 'The change needs a reasoned connection to an intended result.'),
                ('A learning test needs a decision connection.', 'Which fictional test is reviewable?', 'A specified question, observation and decision condition.', ['A prototype celebrated without a question.', 'A real launch without checks.', 'A collection of unrelated metrics.'], 'A clear test explains what learning would change.'),
                ('Traffic and completed access differ.', 'Requests double while processing capacity stays fixed. What remains unresolved?', 'Whether people obtain a usable response.', ['That inclusion is universally achieved.', 'That all requests are automatically completed.', 'That staffing is no longer relevant.'], 'An increase at the front door can coexist with unmet demand.'),
                ('Tool usability does not prove social outcomes.', 'What would overstate the startup assessment study?', 'Calling its artificial usability tests proof of beneficiary improvement.', ['Recording its design-science approach.', 'Acknowledging its limited user testing.', 'Labelling an output proxy honestly.'], 'Testing an instrument is different from testing an enterprise effect.'),
                ('Stopping conditions support responsible learning.', 'What should the fictional expansion proposal include?', 'A review point and conditions for modifying or pausing.', ['Expansion regardless of unresolved capacity.', 'Deletion of adverse observations.', 'Automatic launch authority from coursework.'], 'A bounded test should inform a further authorised decision.')])])
